# Answer 2 · Globalisation and Gender Issues in Developing Countries (CSS 2017)
@py question 2017-6

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Show how globalisation has made gender issues visible, then give opportunities and harms with named country examples, and end with a balanced verdict.
@ask Globalisation has HIGHLIGHTED GENDER ISSUES across cultures
@ask DISCUSS by quoting EXAMPLES from DEVELOPING COUNTRIES
(1) Introduction: globalisation is growing interdependence; it made gender issues visible everywhere
(2) Dimensions: economic, political, technological, cultural and human flows
(3) How issues became visible: global norms, media, supply-chain disasters, migration
(4) Work and income: Bangladesh garments, East Asian electronics, Indian call centres
(5) Feminised and insecure labour: export zones, Mexico's maquiladoras, informal home-based work
(6) Migration and care: Filipina and Sri Lankan domestic workers; global care chains
(7) Health, safety and violence: Rana Plaza, trafficking, harassment at work
(8) Culture and rights: global norms such as CEDAW; backlash and commodified beauty
(9) Pakistan: textiles, home-based workers, remittances, GSP+
(10) Conclusion: globalisation exposes and creates gender issues; policy decides who gains
:::

## Decoding the question
The quotation claims that globalisation has **highlighted** gender issues "across cultures and throughout the globe". **"Discuss"** asks you to explain and weigh this claim, and the instruction to **quote examples from developing countries** is essential: name **countries, industries and events**. Organise by **issue** (work, migration, safety, culture), with examples under each.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Globalisation** is the growing **interdependence of societies** across the world through trade, investment, technology, migration, media and ideas. The sociologist **Anthony Giddens** called it "the intensification of worldwide social relations which link distant localities".

#### ii) The argument
Globalisation has **highlighted** gender issues in two ways: it has made old inequalities **visible** across borders, and it has **created new ones**. At the same time it has opened opportunities. Examples from developing countries show both faces.

### 2. The dimensions of globalisation
Globalisation is **economic** (free trade, multinational companies, global supply chains), **political** (the WTO, IMF and UN; global norms), **technological** (the internet and mobile phones), **cultural** (global media and consumer culture) and **human** (migration of workers). It accelerated from the 1980s with trade liberalisation, the founding of the **World Trade Organization** (==1995==) and the spread of the internet.

### 3. How globalisation made gender issues visible

#### i) Global norms
The **Beijing** conference (==1995==), **CEDAW** and the **SDGs** put violence, discrimination and women's work on every government's agenda.

#### ii) Global media
Television and social media spread news of violence and discrimination instantly; **#MeToo** (==2017==) crossed from the USA to India, South Korea and Pakistan.

#### iii) Supply-chain scrutiny
Consumers and campaigners in rich countries now ask **who made their clothes** — a question that exposes the conditions of women workers. Campaigns such as the Clean Clothes Campaign and, after Rana Plaza, the **Bangladesh Accord on Fire and Building Safety** (==2013==) grew out of this scrutiny.

#### iv) Migration
Millions of women now migrate for work, making their **care labour** and their vulnerability visible in both sending and receiving countries.

### 4. Work and income: new opportunities

#### i) Bangladesh
The garment industry, built for export, employs millions of workers, a large share of them women. For many rural young women it offered a **first independent income**; research by **Naila Kabeer** (*The Power to Choose*, ==2000==) found that garment work delayed marriage and increased women's bargaining power at home.

#### ii) East and South Asia
Electronics factories in **Malaysia** and the **Philippines**, and call centres and IT services in **India**, drew educated young women into paid work, often in cities far from their families.

#### iii) Mobile banking and trade
Mobile money in **Kenya** and online selling across Asia let women earn and save without travelling far. For women whose mobility is restricted, this is a real gain.

### 5. Feminised and insecure labour

#### i) The feminisation of labour
**Guy Standing** (==1989==) described how global competition made jobs **flexible, informal and low-paid** — and increasingly filled by women.

#### ii) Export processing zones
In **Mexico**'s border factories (*maquiladoras*) and in zones across Asia, young women are hired because they are seen as **cheap and docile**; unions are often discouraged, hours are long, and harassment is common.

#### iii) Informal home-based work
Global brands subcontract to home-based workers — embroiderers in **India** and **Pakistan**, garment finishers in **Thailand** — who have no contracts or social security. They carry the risks of the global market — falling orders, late payment — without any of its protections.

### 6. Migration and global care chains

#### i) Domestic workers
Women from the **Philippines**, **Indonesia** and **Sri Lanka** migrate to the Gulf, Hong Kong and Europe as domestic workers and nurses. Their remittances support families, but under sponsorship (*kafala*) systems many face confiscated passports, unpaid wages and abuse.

#### ii) Care chains
**Arlie Hochschild** (==2000==) described **global care chains**: poor women care for richer families' children while their own children are cared for by relatives at home.

### 7. Health, safety and violence

#### i) Unsafe factories
The **Rana Plaza** collapse in **Bangladesh** (==24 April 2013==) killed more than 1,100 garment workers, most of them women. The **Ali Enterprises** fire in Karachi (==2012==) killed about 260.

#### ii) Trafficking
Open borders and global demand fuel **trafficking** of women and girls, for example from **Nepal** to Indian cities and from Eastern Europe and Asia to the Gulf.

#### iii) Harassment at work
Surveys by labour groups in export factories across Asia report frequent verbal and sexual harassment by supervisors — a workplace patriarchy added to the family's.

::: fig compare | Globalisation's two faces for women in developing countries.
## Opportunities
- Export jobs and first independent income (Bangladesh)
- Skilled service jobs (India, Philippines)
- Mobile money and online trade (Kenya, Asia)
- Global rights norms (CEDAW, SDGs)
## Harms
- Cheap, insecure, feminised labour (maquiladoras)
- Informal home-based work (India, Pakistan)
- Migrant abuse and care drain (Philippines, Sri Lanka)
- Unsafe factories and trafficking (Bangladesh, Nepal)
:::

### 8. Culture and rights

#### i) Global rights language
Women's movements in developing countries use **CEDAW**, the **SDGs** and trade conditions to press governments — for example, the EU's **GSP+** trade preferences are tied to human-rights and labour conventions.

#### ii) Backlash and commodification
Global media sell beauty standards such as **skin-lightening** creams across South Asia and Africa. Rapid change can also provoke a conservative reaction that tightens control over women's dress and mobility in the name of protecting local culture. Globalisation thus highlights a clash over **who defines** women's proper place — global norms, local traditions, or women themselves.

### 9. The Pakistan angle
In Pakistan, **textile and garment** exports rely on low-paid workers and **home-based** stitchers; **Sindh** passed the first **Home-Based Workers Act** in ==2018==. Men's migration to the Gulf brings record **remittances** — more than 38 billion dollars in 2024–25 — while many women run households alone. The EU's **GSP+** (from ==2014==) ties trade benefits to conventions including **CEDAW**. Yet female labour-force participation remains ==24.4%==: globalisation has drawn fewer Pakistani women into export jobs than in Bangladesh, because of unsafe transport, restrictions on mobility and the structure of industry. A gendered side effect of global campaigns appeared in **Sialkot**: after the **Atlanta Agreement** (==1997==) against child labour moved football stitching from homes into centres, many women homeworkers who could not travel lost their work.

### 10. Conclusion
The statement is right: globalisation has **highlighted** gender issues across cultures — by exposing violence, exploitation and discrimination to the world, and by creating new forms of work, migration and risk. Examples from Bangladesh, Mexico, the Philippines, India and Pakistan show that globalisation can both **empower and exploit**. Which face women see depends on **labour rights, social protection, safe workplaces and women's own organisation**. Governments of developing countries cannot stop globalisation, but they can shape its gender effects — through minimum wages, factory inspection, protection of migrant and home-based workers, safe transport and childcare.

## Why this answer scores
- Organised by **issue**, with **named developing countries** under each — exactly what the question demands.
- Shows **both opportunities and harms**, then reaches a verdict.
- A **compare figure** summarises the evidence by country.
- Scholars (Giddens, Standing, Hochschild, Kabeer) and events (Rana Plaza, Ali Enterprises) add authority.
